## Event timezone for the availability calendar

### Status
Proposed.

### GitHub Issue
#99

### Summary
Add a per-event IANA timezone so the availability calendar's "today" and candidate days are
interpreted consistently and visibly, instead of implicitly in UTC with no indication to
participants. EventDate values are stored as UTC-midnight of a calendar day with no time-of-day
meaning; this timezone is used purely to decide which local day a stored value represents and
where day-rollover falls for "today"/month-window calculations — no time-of-day slots exist to
convert.

### Motivation
- Nothing today tells participants what timezone the calendar reflects, despite the app committing
  everyone to shared whole-day availability — organizers and participants can't reliably know if
  "the 12th" means their own local the 12th.
- Concretely: `EventsController.Availability.cs`'s `today` is computed from `DateTime.UtcNow`, not
  any event-specific zone, so for several hours around UTC midnight a participant's actual local
  "today" can silently disagree with what the app enforces as the earliest selectable/valid day for
  an event pinned to a non-UTC zone. The month-paging window bounds have the same issue.

---

### Design Decisions
Worked out collaboratively before any code was written:
- **Storage stays UTC.** `EventDate.Date` keeps being stored as UTC-midnight of the chosen day —
  unchanged. The new `Event.TimeZoneId` (IANA id, e.g. `America/New_York`) is a display/
  interpretation lens only, not a new storage format.
- **Always changeable**, not locked in at creation. Editable anytime via the dropdown described
  below, organizer-only.
- **Changing the zone never moves existing dates.** A date stored as "Jan 12" stays "Jan 12" after
  a zone change — it's a relabeling of which zone that day is interpreted in, not a UTC-instant
  conversion that could shift it to Jan 11 or Jan 13. A plain-language confirmation dialog (no
  timezone jargon) is shown before the change saves, since this distinction is easy to get wrong
  intuitively; canceling leaves the previous zone in place.
- **Default:** new events pre-fill the organizer's browser-detected zone (editable before create);
  falls back to UTC if detection fails. Existing events are backfilled to UTC on migration — matches
  today's implicit behavior, so nothing changes for them until an organizer explicitly repicks.
- **Picker UI:** a plain grouped native `<select>` (`<optgroup>` per continent/area, options sorted
  by UTC offset, labeled `(UTC−05:00) America/New_York`), populated server-side from
  `TimeZoneInfo.GetSystemTimeZones()` — the full canonical IANA zone list. No new frontend
  dependency: a JS typeahead/combobox was considered and deliberately deferred (see Out of Scope)
  since the native grouped select is fully accessible and .NET's zone list needs no library to
  render or keep current.
- **Permission level:** gated the same as other event-detail edits (`CanManageEventAsync` via
  `AuthorizeEventActionAsync`), not the stricter `CanManageOrganizers` tier.
- **Folded-in bug fix:** "today" and the month-paging window bounds move from `DateTime.UtcNow` to
  the event's timezone, since it's the same underlying day-interpretation concern.

---

### Proposed Changes

#### Schema
- New `Event.TimeZoneId` (string, IANA id) column + EF Core migration. Backfill existing rows to
  `"UTC"`.

#### Server
- `EventsController.Availability.cs`: `today` and the boundary check use the event's `TimeZoneId`
  (via `TimeZoneInfo.FindSystemTimeZoneById` + `TimeZoneInfo.ConvertTime`) instead of
  `DateTime.UtcNow` directly.
- `EventsController.Home.cs`: `InitialMonth`/`WindowStartMonth`/`WindowEndMonth` computed the same
  way.
- New organizer-gated endpoint (alongside `EventsController.Settings.cs`'s pattern) to update
  `Event.TimeZoneId`, authorized via `AuthorizeEventActionAsync(code, CanManageEventAsync, ...)`.
- A server-side helper builds the grouped, offset-sorted zone list from
  `TimeZoneInfo.GetSystemTimeZones()` for the dropdown.

#### Client
- Dropdown added to the Availability tab's calendar card header (`ww-calendar-card` in
  `Views/Events/Home.cshtml`), near the existing subtitle.
- Browser timezone detection (`Intl.DateTimeFormat().resolvedOptions().timeZone`) pre-fills the
  select on event creation.
- Confirmation dialog (plain language, no jargon) on change, before submit; cancel reverts the
  select to the previous value without saving.

---

### Acceptance Criteria
- An event's calendar clearly displays its current timezone near the calendar, visible to every
  participant (not just organizers).
- Only a participant with organizer permissions (`CanManageEventAsync`) can change it; the control
  is hidden or disabled for non-organizers.
- The dropdown lists the full canonical IANA zone set, grouped by continent/area and sorted by UTC
  offset, each option showing its current UTC offset alongside its id.
- Creating a new event pre-fills the organizer's browser-detected zone; if detection fails, UTC.
- Changing the zone shows a plain-language confirmation dialog before saving; canceling makes no
  change. Confirming updates `Event.TimeZoneId` without altering any existing `EventDate` row's
  stored day.
- After a zone change, "today"/the selectable date range and month-paging window reflect the new
  zone, not UTC.
- Pre-existing events read as UTC immediately after migration, with no visible change until an
  organizer explicitly picks a different zone.

### Out of Scope
- Chat message timestamps (`EventMessage.SentAt`) and `Event.CreatedAt`/`LastActiveAt` display —
  chat has no controller actions/views yet, and neither is part of the day-picking flow.
- A JS typeahead/combobox timezone picker — may be revisited later as an additive UX upgrade to the
  same field; not needed for this pass.
- Time-of-day availability slots — the calendar remains whole-day granularity; this issue only
  fixes which day a stored value/`"today"` maps to.
- Tests — per CLAUDE.md, added only on separate explicit request.
